# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MAP ONLY -- there is no root .env. Do not create one.
#
# Each app owns its own env file, next to the code that reads it. This keeps
# `npm run dev` / `manage.py runserver` and Docker reading the SAME file, so
# the two can never drift apart.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   backend/.env            Django + all server-side secrets
#     from: backend/.env.example
#     read by: config/settings.py (BASE_DIR/.env), and docker-compose `env_file:`
#     holds: SECRET_KEY, DB_*, CORS/CSRF, GOOGLE_* (incl. private key), EMAIL_*
#
#   frontend/hub/.env       Vite vars for Hub (dev server :3000)
#     from: frontend/hub/.env.example
#     read by: `npm run dev` and `npm run build` (also inside Docker)
#     holds: VITE_API_URL, VITE_GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID, VITE_HUB_URL, VITE_LEARN_URL
#
#   frontend/learn/.env     Vite vars for Learn (dev server :3001)
#     from: frontend/learn/.env.example
#     same four VITE_* keys as Hub
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets boundary
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything VITE_* is compiled into the browser bundle and is PUBLIC.
# The Google OAuth *client id* is public by design and appears in both places.
# The Google *private key*, the Gmail app password and SECRET_KEY are
# server-side only and must appear ONLY in backend/.env.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# First-time setup
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   cp backend/.env.example        backend/.env
#   cp frontend/hub/.env.example   frontend/hub/.env
#   cp frontend/learn/.env.example frontend/learn/.env
#
# Postgres container credentials are literals in docker-compose.yml; if you
# change DB_USER/DB_PASSWORD in backend/.env, change them there to match.
